  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Clavulina castaneipes is a small, branched fungus in the Hydnaceae family, characterized by its brown to chestnut-colored stem and delicate, fork-like fruiting body. This woodland fungus is commonly found in New England forests and plays a role in decomposition and nutrient cycling.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Chestnut-colored stem",
      "description": "The distinctive brown to chestnut-colored base and stalk is the most diagnostic feature of this species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Forked branches",
      "description": "The fruiting body has multiple delicate, repeatedly forked branches that resemble miniature antlers"
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "This fungus typically grows only 2-6 cm tall, making it a relatively small and delicate structure"
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Clavulina castaneipes thrives in moist, wooded areas of New England, particularly in rich soil and leaf litter on the forest floor.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Members of the Clavulina genus are sometimes called \"club fungi\" due to their club or coral-like appearance, and they often form mycorrhizal relationships with surrounding trees.",
